<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fujiwara no Mototsune</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">836–<a href="/years/891">891</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Horikawa Daijin, Shōsen Kō</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fujiwara no Mototsune was a Japanese statesman, courtier and aristocrat of the early Heian period and the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sessho-and-kampaku">kampaku</a>, a regent for an adult emperor, in Japanese history. Born the third son of Fujiwara no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bednur">Nagara</a>, he was adopted by his powerful uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-yoshifusa">Fujiwara no Yoshifusa</a>, who had no sons, and followed him as regent, holding power at court for four successive emperors. Named sesshō in 876 and Daijō Daijin in 880, he invented the office of kampaku for himself so that he could stay in power after an emperor came of age, receiving the title first in 884. The innovation let the Fujiwara tighten their grip on power throughout an emperor's reign. He died in 891.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-mototsune.jpg" alt="Fujiwara no Mototsune" width="330" height="351" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kikuchi Yōsai · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fujiwara_no_Mototsune.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
